\begin{proposition}[The smooth-multiple assertion of Remark 7.2]\label{cdp:st:A.3.4}
\StatementRecord{cdp:st:A.3.4}{proved}{Historical records: GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode), effort Pro, 17 November 2026; Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort high, 16 November 2026. Also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker. proved. Complete normalized root-cover, unitary local-system and nilpotent-layer argument; generic simultaneous scope must explicitly specify m\ensuremath{\geq}2. The normalized charts w\textasciicircum{}m=u, s=wz are finite étale over U and make the central fibre reduced with trivial conormal. Nearby fibre restriction maps are isomorphisms. Rechecked the calling roles of D.1.9, A.4.7 and A.4.9: the irrational class is nonzero, twisted H0/H1 vanish, and the closed-form primitive injection kills holomorphic differentials. Exact ideal-power induction kills all m layers, and trace/m injects the original sections. The analytic finite-union argument is exact for the finitely many smooth multiple fibres m\ensuremath{\geq}2; it alone does not establish a uniform generic locus over every smooth regular fibre. Review provenance: New derivation after reading the supplied proof; not a blind review and not an independent human check. The receiving statement now expressly restricts the multiple-fibre and simultaneous-generic clauses to multiplicity at least two}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cref{cdp:st:D.1.9}; \cref{cdp:st:A.4.9}; \cref{cdp:st:A.4.7}; \cref{cdp:st:A.3.1}}

Under the hypotheses of CDP20 Proposition~7.3, let $mS$, with $m\ge2$, be a full fibre whose reduced support $S$ is smooth. For every real irrational transgression parameter $r$ of \ref{cdp:st:D.1.9},
\[
 H^0\bigl(mS,(\Omega_Y^1\otimes L_r)|_{mS}\bigr)=0.
 \tag{A.15a}\label{cdp:eq:A.15a}
\]
The same simultaneous vanishing on all smooth multiple fibres $(m\ge2)$ holds on the complement of a proper complex analytic subset of $\Pic(Y)$. The assertion includes the entire scheme-theoretic fibre, with every nilpotent layer.
\end{proposition}

cdp:st:A.5.2:
\begin{proposition}[Normal Gorenstein surfaces with vanishing first Chern class and Euler characteristic]\label{cdp:st:A.5.2}
\StatementRecord{cdp:st:A.5.2}{proved}{Historical records: GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode), effort Pro, 17 November 2026; Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort high, 2026-11-16; Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort max, 16 November 2026. Also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker. The ruled-resolution Euler contradiction, rational singularity case, crepant negative-definite discrepancy calculation, and the c1=c2=0 classification consequences were re-derived}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cite[Theorem~2.2]{Peters2021}}

A compact connected normal Gorenstein complex surface $S$ with $c_1(\omega_S)=0$ in $H^2(S,\Q)$ and $\chi(S,\OO_S)=0$ is smooth. Consequently every singular irreducible reduced divisor in a compact complex threefold $Y$ with $H^2(Y,\Q)=0$ is nonnormal. In particular the assertion of CDP20 Proposition~4.11 holds without its monodromy-dependent restrictions on the smooth fibre.
\end{proposition}

cdp:st:A.5.3:
\begin{proposition}[The effective conductor divisor on a minimal resolution]\label{cdp:st:A.5.3}
\StatementRecord{cdp:st:A.5.3}{proved}{Historical records: GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode), effort Pro, 17 November 2026; Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort max, 16 November 2026. Also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker. Finite-duality conductor effectiveness, the exceptional intersection-matrix equation, and the horizontal degree-two calculation were re-derived}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cref{cdp:st:A.2.1}; \cite[Lemma~1.1 and Corollary~1.4]{Furushima1993}}

Let $S$ be a compact reduced hypersurface in a smooth threefold, let $\eta:\coprod_i T_i\to S$ be its normalization, and let $\pi_i:Z_i\to T_i$ be the minimal resolutions. Put $h_i=\eta\pi_i$. There are canonical effective integral divisors $D_i$ on $Z_i$ with
\[
 \omega_{Z_i}(D_i)\simeq h_i^*\omega_S.
 \tag{A.25}\label{cdp:eq:A.25}
\]
The nonexceptional part of $D_i$ is exactly the divisorial conductor, with its scheme-theoretic multiplicities. When $S\subset Y$ and $H^2(Y,\Q)=0$, every $K_{Z_i}+D_i$ has rational first Chern class zero. A ruled $Z_i\to B$ with $g(B)\ge2$ then has
\[
 D_i=2C+V,
 \tag{A.26}\label{cdp:eq:A.26}
\]
where $C$ is a section and $V$ is vertical. In the case where $C$ is $\pi_i$-exceptional, $\eta^*(L|_S)|_{T_i}$ is trivial for every $L\in\Pic(Y)$.
\end{proposition}

